{"name":"Spätzle Yellow","hex":"#ffee88","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"spätzle","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of spaetzle."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sp%C3%A4tzle"},{"term":"yellow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of sunflower petals and lemons; the color obtained by mixing green and red light, or by subtracting blue from white light; the color evoked by light of wavelength around 580 nm; one of the three primary colors in subtractive color systems.","The middle light in a set of three traffic lights, the lighting of which indicates that drivers should stop short of the intersection if it is safe to do so."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a yellow hue.","Lacking courage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/yellow"}],"entries":[{"title":"Spätzle","blocks":[{"text":"Spätzle, Spätzla Knöpfle or Spatzen, also called nokedli, are a type of egg pasta typically served as a side for meat dishes with sauce. Commonly associated with Swabia and Alsace, it is also found in the cuisines of southern Germany and Austria, Switzerland, Liechtenstein, Lorraine, Moselle, Trentino and South Tyrol. It may be served as a side dish or with other ingredients like cheese and onion as a main dish. Spätzle are egg-based pasta of an irregular form with a rough, porous surface. The glutinous dough is put directly into boiling water or steam and the form varies between thin and thick, elongated and short. Spätzle is cooked for the first time during the fabrication. The moist dough is either pressed through a perforated metal plate or it drips through this plate into the boiling water. Other ways to prepare Spätzle are more applicable for domestic use."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Sp%C3%A4tzle-02.jpg/330px-Sp%C3%A4tzle-02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sp%C3%A4tzle"},{"title":"Yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Yellow is a color between green and orange on the spectrum of light. It is evoked by light with a dominant wavelength of roughly 575–585 nm. It is a primary color in subtractive color systems, used in painting or color printing. In the RGB color model, used to create colors on television and computer screens, yellow is a secondary color made by combining red and green at equal intensity. Carotenoids give the characteristic yellow color to autumn leaves, corn, canaries, daffodils, and lemons, as well as egg yolks, buttercups, and bananas. They absorb light energy and protect plants from photo damage in some cases. Sunlight has a slight yellowish hue when the Sun is near the horizon, due to atmospheric scattering of shorter wavelengths."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg/330px-Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellow"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Spaetzleschaben_a2_21.10.2011_19-51-33.jpg/500px-Spaetzleschaben_a2_21.10.2011_19-51-33.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Spaetzleschaben_a2_21.10.2011_19-51-33.jpg/1280px-Spaetzleschaben_a2_21.10.2011_19-51-33.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Spaetzleschaben_a2_21.10.2011_19-51-33.jpg/500px-Spaetzleschaben_a2_21.10.2011_19-51-33.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Manual process by scraping from a board","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpaetzleschaben_a2_21.10.2011_19-51-33.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Spaetzlehobel.jpg/500px-Spaetzlehobel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Spaetzlehobel.jpg/1280px-Spaetzlehobel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Spaetzlehobel.jpg/500px-Spaetzlehobel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A \"hopper\" type Spätzle maker (Spätzlehobel)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpaetzlehobel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Sp%C3%A4tzlepresse2.jpg/500px-Sp%C3%A4tzlepresse2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Sp%C3%A4tzlepresse2.jpg/1280px-Sp%C3%A4tzlepresse2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Sp%C3%A4tzlepresse2.jpg/500px-Sp%C3%A4tzlepresse2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A \"potato ricer\" type Spätzle maker (Spätzlepresse)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASp%C3%A4tzlepresse2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Paprikahuhn.jpg/500px-Paprikahuhn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Paprikahuhn.jpg/1280px-Paprikahuhn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Paprikahuhn.jpg/500px-Paprikahuhn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hungarian chicken paprikash with Spätzle (Hungarian nokedli)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APaprikahuhn.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Sp%C3%A4tzle.jpg/250px-Sp%C3%A4tzle.jpg?utm_